     "text": [
      "RAG stands for **Retrieval-Augmented Generation**. It’s a way to make a language model answer questions using **external knowledge** instead of relying only on what it memorized during training.\n",
      "\n",
      "In simple terms:\n",
      "\n",
      "1. **Retrieve** relevant information from a knowledge source\n",
      "2. **Augment** the model’s input with that information\n",
      "3. **Generate** the final answer using both the retrieved context and the model’s reasoning\n",
      "\n",
      "---\n",
      "\n",
      "## Why RAG exists\n",
      "\n",
      "Large language models are good at:\n",
      "- understanding language\n",
      "- summarizing\n",
      "- reasoning over provided text\n",
      "\n",
      "But they have limits:\n",
      "- they can be **out of date**\n",
      "- they may **hallucinate** facts\n",
      "- they don’t know your private documents unless you give them access\n",
      "- retraining the model every time new information arrives is expensive\n",
      "\n",
      "RAG solves this by letting the model **look things up** before answering.\n",
      "\n",
      "---\n",
      "\n",
      "## Core idea\n",
      "\n",
      "Instead of asking:\n",
      "\n",
      "> “What does the model already know?”\n",
      "\n",
      "RAG asks:\n",
      "\n",
      "> “What relevant information can I fetch first, then use to answer?”\n",
      "\n",
      "This makes LLMs more useful for:\n",
      "- company knowledge bases\n",
      "- support chatbots\n",
      "- legal/medical document search\n",
      "- product documentation assistants\n",
      "- research tools\n",
      "- question answering over PDFs, web pages, or databases\n",
      "\n",
      "---\n",
      "\n",
      "## High-level pipeline\n",
      "\n",
      "A typical RAG system has two main stages:\n",
      "\n",
      "### 1. Retrieval\n",
      "Given a user query, the system finds relevant documents or passages from a corpus.\n",
      "\n",
      "### 2. Generation\n",
      "The retrieved passages are added to the prompt, and the LLM generates an answer grounded in them.\n",
      "\n",
      "---\n",
      "\n",
      "## Step-by-step workflow\n",
      "\n",
      "### 1. Ingest documents\n",
      "You start with sources such as:\n",
      "- PDFs\n",
      "- wiki pages\n",
      "- websites\n",
      "- tickets\n",
      "- manuals\n",
      "- database rows\n",
      "- emails\n",
      "\n",
      "These sources are converted into text and often split into smaller chunks.\n",
      "\n",
      "---\n",
      "\n",
      "### 2. Chunk the text\n",
      "Documents are usually split into manageable pieces, called **chunks**.\n",
      "\n",
      "Why chunk?\n",
      "- LLMs have context limits\n",
      "- retrieval works better on smaller units\n",
      "- smaller chunks are easier to match to a query\n",
      "\n",
      "Example:\n",
      "- a 20-page manual might be split into 300–800 token chunks\n",
      "- chunks may overlap a little to preserve context\n",
      "\n",
      "---\n",
      "\n",
      "### 3. Create embeddings\n",
      "Each chunk is converted into a vector representation using an **embedding model**.\n",
      "\n",
      "An embedding is a numeric vector that captures semantic meaning.\n",
      "\n",
      "For example:\n",
      "- “How do I reset my password?”\n",
      "- “Steps to change your login credentials”\n",
      "\n",
      "These may be close in embedding space because they mean similar things.\n",
      "\n",
      "---\n",
      "\n",
      "### 4. Store embeddings in a vector database\n",
      "The chunk embeddings are stored in a **vector index** or **vector database** such as:\n",
      "- FAISS\n",
      "- Pinecone\n",
      "- Weaviate\n",
      "- Milvus\n",
      "- Chroma\n",
      "- Elasticsearch/OpenSearch vector search\n",
      "\n",
      "This allows fast similarity search.\n",
      "\n",
      "---\n",
      "\n",
      "### 5. Receive a user query\n",
      "When the user asks a question, the query is also embedded.\n",
      "\n",
      "Example:\n",
      "> “How do I reset my password?”\n",
      "\n",
      "The query embedding is compared to stored chunk embeddings.\n",
      "\n",
      "---\n",
      "\n",
      "### 6. Retrieve top relevant chunks\n",
      "The system returns the top-k most similar chunks.\n",
      "\n",
      "These may come from:\n",
      "- semantic similarity search\n",
      "- keyword search\n",
      "- hybrid search\n",
      "- filtered search by metadata\n",
      "- reranking with a cross-encoder or LLM\n",
      "\n",
      "---\n",
      "\n",
      "### 7. Add retrieved text to the prompt\n",
      "The retrieved chunks are inserted into the prompt given to the LLM.\n",
      "\n",
      "For example:\n",
      "\n",
      "**System/Instruction:**  \n",
      "Answer only using the provided context.\n",
      "\n",
      "**Context:**  \n",
      "Chunk 1...  \n",
      "Chunk 2...  \n",
      "Chunk 3...\n",
      "\n",
      "**Question:**  \n",
      "How do I reset my password?\n",
      "\n",
      "---\n",
      "\n",
      "### 8. Generate the answer\n",
      "The LLM generates a response based on:\n",
      "- the retrieved context\n",
      "- the user’s question\n",
      "- its own language ability\n",
      "\n",
      "A good RAG system tries to ensure the answer is **grounded** in retrieved sources.\n",
      "\n",
      "---\n",
      "\n",
      "## Why RAG works well\n",
      "\n",
      "RAG has several benefits:\n",
      "\n",
      "### 1. More up-to-date answers\n",
      "You can update the knowledge base without retraining the model.\n",
      "\n",
      "### 2. Domain-specific knowledge\n",
      "You can use it on private or specialized data.\n",
      "\n",
      "### 3. Better factuality\n",
      "The model has relevant source material to lean on, reducing hallucinations.\n",
      "\n",
      "### 4. Transparency\n",
      "You can show citations or source passages.\n",
      "\n",
      "### 5. Lower cost than retraining\n",
      "Updating documents is usually cheaper than fine-tuning or full retraining.\n",
      "\n",
      "---\n",
      "\n",
      "## RAG vs fine-tuning\n",
      "\n",
      "These are often confused, but they solve different problems.\n",
      "\n",
      "### RAG\n",
      "Best for:\n",
      "- factual knowledge\n",
      "- changing information\n",
      "- private documents\n",
      "- traceability\n",
      "\n",
      "RAG changes the **input context**.\n",
      "\n",
      "### Fine-tuning\n",
      "Best for:\n",
      "- style\n",
      "- format\n",
      "- behavior\n",
      "- specialized task patterns\n",
      "\n",
      "Fine-tuning changes the **model weights**.\n",
      "\n",
      "### Rule of thumb\n",
      "- If the information changes often, use **RAG**\n",
      "- If you want the model to behave differently, use **fine-tuning**\n",
      "- Many systems use both\n",
      "\n",
      "---\n",
      "\n",
      "## Example\n",
      "\n",
      "Suppose you build a chatbot for HR policies.\n",
      "\n",
      "User asks:\n",
      "> “How many vacation days do new employees get?”\n",
      "\n",
      "A RAG pipeline might:\n",
      "1. search the HR handbook\n",
      "2. retrieve the policy section\n",
      "3. give that text to the LLM\n",
      "4. generate:\n",
      "   > “New employees receive 15 vacation days per year during their first two years.”\n",
      "\n",
      "Without RAG, the model might guess or hallucinate.\n",
      "\n",
      "---\n",
      "\n",
      "## Important components in RAG\n",
      "\n",
      "### 1. Retrieval method\n",
      "Common retrieval methods:\n",
      "- **Dense retrieval**: embeddings + vector similarity\n",
      "- **Sparse retrieval**: keyword search like BM25\n",
      "- **Hybrid retrieval**: combines both\n",
      "\n",
      "Dense retrieval is good for semantic matching.  \n",
      "Sparse retrieval is good when exact terms matter.\n",
      "\n",
      "---\n",
      "\n",
      "### 2. Chunking strategy\n",
      "Chunking affects performance a lot.\n",
      "\n",
      "Things to consider:\n",
      "- chunk size\n",
      "- overlap\n",
      "- document structure\n",
      "- preserving headings and tables\n",
      "- splitting by semantic boundaries\n",
      "\n",
      "Bad chunking can lead to irrelevant or incomplete retrieval.\n",
      "\n",
      "---\n",
      "\n",
      "### 3. Ranking / reranking\n",
      "Often the initial retrieval gets many candidates, then a reranker sorts them by actual relevance.\n",
      "\n",
      "This improves precision.\n",
      "\n",
      "---\n",
      "\n",
      "### 4. Prompt engineering\n",
      "The way retrieved text is inserted into the prompt matters:\n",
      "- provide instructions to use only context\n",
      "- ask for citations\n",
      "- tell the model to say “I don’t know” if context is insufficient\n",
      "- format context clearly\n",
      "\n",
      "---\n",
      "\n",
      "### 5. Context window management\n",
      "LLMs have limited context length, so you may need:\n",
      "- fewer chunks\n",
      "- summaries\n",
      "- compression\n",
      "- reranking\n",
      "- iterative retrieval\n",
      "\n",
      "---\n",
      "\n",
      "## Common RAG variants\n",
      "\n",
      "### 1. Naive RAG\n",
      "Simple pipeline:\n",
      "- retrieve top-k chunks\n",
      "- pass to LLM\n",
      "\n",
      "Easy to build, but may be brittle.\n",
      "\n",
      "### 2. Advanced RAG\n",
      "Adds:\n",
      "- query rewriting\n",
      "- hybrid search\n",
      "- reranking\n",
      "- filtering\n",
      "- multi-step retrieval\n",
      "\n",
      "### 3. Multi-hop RAG\n",
      "Used when answering requires multiple retrieval steps.\n",
      "\n",
      "Example:\n",
      "- retrieve a product\n",
      "- then retrieve its policy\n",
      "- then combine them to answer\n",
      "\n",
      "### 4. Graph RAG\n",
      "Uses a knowledge graph or entity relationships to improve retrieval over connected facts.\n",
      "\n",
      "### 5. Agentic RAG\n",
      "An agent decides when and how to retrieve, possibly multiple times.\n",
      "\n",
      "### 6. Conversational RAG\n",
      "Uses chat history and dialogue state to retrieve based on the conversation.\n",
      "\n",
      "---\n",
      "\n",
      "## Challenges and failure modes\n",
      "\n",
      "RAG is powerful, but not magic.\n",
      "\n",
      "### 1. Bad retrieval\n",
      "If the system retrieves the wrong documents, the answer will be wrong.\n",
      "\n",
      "### 2. Missing context\n",
      "Important information may not be in the top results.\n",
      "\n",
      "### 3. Chunking issues\n",
      "A key sentence may be split away from its surrounding meaning.\n",
      "\n",
      "### 4. Hallucination despite retrieval\n",
      "The model may still invent details even with context.\n",
      "\n",
      "### 5. Conflicting sources\n",
      "Different documents may disagree.\n",
      "\n",
      "### 6. Prompt injection\n",
      "Retrieved documents may contain malicious instructions like:\n",
      "> “Ignore previous instructions”\n",
      "\n",
      "This is a security risk in RAG systems.\n",
      "\n",
      "### 7. Evaluation is hard\n",
      "It’s not enough to check whether the answer sounds good; you need to know whether retrieval was correct and whether the answer is grounded.\n",
      "\n",
      "---\n",
      "\n",
      "## Evaluating a RAG system\n",
      "\n",
      "Typical metrics and checks:\n",
      "\n",
      "### Retrieval quality\n",
      "- Recall@k\n",
      "- Precision@k\n",
      "- Mean Reciprocal Rank (MRR)\n",
      "- nDCG\n",
      "\n",
      "### Generation quality\n",
      "- correctness\n",
      "- faithfulness to source\n",
      "- citation accuracy\n",
      "- completeness\n",
      "- helpfulness\n",
      "\n",
      "### End-to-end quality\n",
      "- does the final answer actually solve the user’s task?\n",
      "\n",
      "You often need human evaluation plus automated tests.\n",
      "\n",
      "---\n",
      "\n",
      "## Best practices\n",
      "\n",
      "- Use **hybrid search** when possible\n",
      "- Chunk thoughtfully, not arbitrarily\n",
      "- Add metadata filters\n",
      "- Rerank retrieved results\n",
      "- Ask the model to cite sources\n",
      "- Instruct it to say when context is insufficient\n",
      "- Monitor and log retrieval results\n",
      "- Protect against prompt injection\n",
      "- Evaluate retrieval and generation separately\n",
      "- Keep your knowledge base fresh\n",
      "\n",
      "---\n",
      "\n",
      "## Simple mental model\n",
      "\n",
      "Think of RAG as:\n",
      "- a **search engine** + a **writer**\n",
      "- the search engine fetches evidence\n",
      "- the writer turns evidence into a natural answer\n",
      "\n",
      "---\n",
      "\n",
      "## In one sentence\n",
      "\n",
      "**RAG is a method that improves LLM responses by retrieving relevant external information and feeding it into the model before generation.**\n",
      "\n",
      "If you want, I can also give you:\n",
      "1. a **diagram of the RAG architecture**,  \n",
      "2. a **Python example of a basic RAG pipeline**, or  \n",
      "3. a **comparison of RAG vs fine-tuning vs agents**."
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "for chunk in model.stream(\"Explain RAG in detail.\"):\n",
